capsules may sometimes be described as enteric-coated or as delayed-release), prolonged-release or another modified-release form-
Granules
Preparations that are issued to patient as granules to
be swallowed without
further preparation, to be chewed, or to be taken in or with water oranother suitable liquid.The term'granules'without further qualification is never intended to allowany type of modified-release granules.
Oral powder
Preparations that are issued to patient as powder (usually as
single-dose)
to be taken in or with water or another suitable liquid.
Oralliquid
Liquid preparations intended to be swallowed i.e. oral
solutions,
suspensions, emulsions and oral drops, including those constituted frompowders or granules, but nof those preparations intended for oromucosaladministration e.g. gargles and mouthwast,es.Oral liquids presented as powders or granules may offer benefits in theform of better stability and lower transport costs. lf more than one type oforal liquid is available on the same market (e.9. solution, suspension,granules for reconstitution), they may be interchanged and in such casesshould be bioequivalent. lt is preferable that oral liquids do not containsugar and that solutions for children do not contain alcohol.
